  "strategy": "[1. Approach and rationale]\n### Central idea\nThe Erd\u0151s problem requires finding a step function h on [0,2] that minimizes the maximum overlap with its shifted complement. The core idea is to parameterize h using a multi-resolution basis that can efficiently capture structural features while trading off between numerical precision and computational cost. This approach dynamically adjusts the grid density across intervals, concentrating higher resolution where optimization progress stalls, rather than using uniform resolution throughout.\n\n### Rationale\nCurrent implementations focus on fixed grid optimization with gradient descent, which often gets stuck in local minima. The basis-encoding approach allows for adaptive optimization by:\n- Capturing hierarchical features (low-frequency/monotonic shapes first)\n- Maintaining continuity across resolutions\n- Automatically regularizing solutions\n- Allowing efficient refinement where needed\n\nThe method starts with minimal resolution (basis size) and increases only when optimization stalls, balancing detail with time. This leverages the observation that optimal solutions often exhibit certain structural regularity (monotonicity, multi-scale features) that can be captured progressively.\n\n### Limitations & Differences\n- No gradient calculations needed\n- Requires careful handling of multi-resolution optimization\n- Assumes basis functions can parameterize all feasible h well enough\n- Solidifies the integral constraint upfront rather than during optimization\n\n[2. Mathematical specification]\n### Representation\nRepresent h as a linear combination of B-spline basis functions:\n\\begin{align*}\nh(x) = \\sum_{j=1}^{N} \\beta_j B_j(x)\n\\end{align*}\nwhere:\n- \u03b2 is a vector of coefficients \u2192 $|\\beta| \\approx \\frac{1}{2}$\n- $B_j$ are normalized cubic B-splines \u2192 $\\int_0^2 B_j(x)dx = \\frac{1}{N}$\n- N is global basis size parameter\n- Start with N_base, increase to N_max if needed\n\n### Objective\nTransform the maximum correlation into a smooth function:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\max_k C_k &= \\max_k \\| h \\odot (1-h(\\cdot +k)) \\|_{L2}\n\\end{align*}\nSubstitute h and use exponential tilting to make differentiable:\n\\begin{align*}\nF(\u03b2, \u03c4) &= \\min_{\u03b2} \\log \\sum_{k} \\exp\\left[ \\beta^T \\Phi_k - \\max_b \\beta^T \\Phi_b \\right] + \\text{constraints}\n\\end{align*}\nwhere $\\Phi$ encodes correlation features.\n\n### Constraints (handled by Lagrange)\n- Integral constraint: $\\Lambda_\\text{int} = |\\int h(x)dx - 0.5|$\n- Bound constraints: $\\Lambda_\\text{bounds} = \\| h - [0,1] \\|_2$\n\n### Solver specification\n- Projected Stochastic Gradient Descent (PSGD) with adaptive momentum\n- Momentum parameter \u03bc \u2208 (0,1), step size \u03b1\n- Loss function: $L(\u03b2) = F(\u03b2) + \\epsilon \\cdot (\\Lambda_\\text{int} + \\|\\beta\\|_1 + \\epsilon^2 \\| \\nabla F(\u03b2) \\|^2)$\n- Regularization forces rapid boundary escape\n- Projection step ensures Fejer monotonicity\n\n[Implementation procedure]\n3.1 Initialization\n- Start with basis N0 = ceil(n_initial^(2/3))\n- Generate random \u03b20 in [-\u03b7, \u03b7] with constraint enforcement\n- Set global basis size N_global = ceil(n_initial^(1/2)) as upper bound\n\n3.2 Core update loop\nwhile time_budget_remaining > \u03b5 AND n_iter < MAX_ITERATIONS:\n    Compute feature correlations for full set of shifts\n    Identify bottleneck shift k*\n    Solve: $\\tilde{\\beta} = \\arg\\min_{\\beta} \\| \\beta - \\beta_\\text{old} - \\alpha t \\nabla F(\\beta) \\|_2$\n    Set $\\beta = \\beta + \\mu (\\beta_\\text{tilt} - \\beta_\\text{old})$\n    Clip coefficients via \u03b2_new = clip(\u03b2_old - c, lower_bound, upper_bound)\n    Adjust basis size if gradient norm < 1e-5*|\u03a9|\n\n    Project to feasible space:\n    - If integral error > 0.5*|\u03a9|, expand basis by doubling\n    - If stationarity detected, refine basis locally (basis doubling near identified issues)\n\n3.3 Constraint enforcement (Lagrangian added to loss)\nUse exponential weighted loss: $L(\\beta) = \\log(\\sum_k \\exp(\\beta^T \\Phi_k / \u03c4))$\n\n3.4 Specialized projection operator\nGiven overcomplete representation, project by:\n- Scaling \u03b2 to satisfy integral roughly\n- Applying single projection step to clip bound-violations\n- Using cyclic adjustment for basis expansion\n\n[4. Search choices and variations]\n### Fixed parameters (default)\n- Basis doubling coefficient (\u03bc_basis) \u2208 (0.8, 1.0): defaults to 1.2\n- Local expansion factor (\u03c1_local) \u2208 (0.8, 1.0): defaults to 1.25\n- Regularization strength (\u03b7) \u2208 (1e-3, 1e-1): starts at 5e-3\n- Initial basis size: floor(50*(log(n_parent)), e.g., 250 for n_parent=900\n\n### Dynamic adjustments\n- Check Stabilization: if norm(\u2207F) < 1e-5 for 100 steps, double basis\n- Progress threshold: if best_C improvement < 1e-5 for 500 steps, double basis\n- Override: user-defined max/MIN basis sizes\n\n### Variations\n- Basis type: switch between Chebyshev vs B-spline basis subsets\n- Loss transformation: switch between hinge vs exponential loss\n- Optimize \u03b3 distribution to inject non-stationarity\n\n[5. Compute budget and robustness]\n#### Dominant costs\n- Basis evaluation: mathematically exact using FFTs, O(n log n)\n- Correlation width: limited to characteristic correlation length\n- Optimization pace: one full optimization per basis expansion\n\n#### Time allocation\n- Safe execution: ~600 seconds for main optimization, 200 for checking validity\n- Resource check: if machine < 20G memory, switch to sparse mode\n- Death condition: if time_remaining < 30, return best so far\n\n#### Recovery chain\n- Backup: maintain h_old at solution archive\n- Pilot optimization: lower resolution h_old_ref with ~2x speed\n- Re-radius: expand basis only gradually (factor of 2, not 3)\n- Recovery: piecewise linear interpolation from nearby viable solution\n\n[6. Final validation]\nAfter returning to user:\n- Re-evaluate best_h on training system\n- Check: \n    - Integral: abs(np.sum(best_h)*dx - 0.5) < 1e-3\n    - Correlation: max_corr = (compute_corr(best_h).max())/n\n    - Bounds: np.all((0 <= best_h <= 1))\n- Format output: (best_h, best_C5, n_points_actual)\n\n[A complete implementation of this algorithm remains to be written; this plan provides the mathematical framework and optimization structure. Proceed with deriving updates to the basis, projection steps, and loss functions.]",
